>
<h1>Bucket Elevators</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A bucket elevator lifts bulk materials vertically using a series of buckets attached to a belt or chain that loops around head (top) and boot (bottom) pulleys inside an enclosed casing. They are a common choice for vertical bulk transport of grain, cement, ore, chemicals, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food ingredients</a> because they lift straight up in a small footprint. The main discharge types, centrifugal, continuous, and positive (with internal discharge as a less common variant), suit different material characteristics and throughput requirements.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/bucket-sweet-grain-leg.webp" width="1000" height="882" alt="A tall galvanized bucket elevator with two enclosed vertical legs, ladders, and a service platform at the head stands between two corrugated steel grain bins, with spouts running down from the top to the bins." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A bucket elevator at a grain storage site. The buckets travel inside the enclosed vertical legs, up one and down the other, and material discharges at the head, where spouts carry it down to the bins. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.sweetmfg.com/bucket-elevators" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sweet Manufacturing</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="centrifugal-heading">Centrifugal Discharge Elevators</h2>
<p>Centrifugal discharge bucket elevators run at high speed. Todd Smith of Kansas State University's Bulk Solids Technology Center puts typical belt speeds at 225 to 300 feet per minute, and up to 500 fpm in some designs (<a href="https://www.processingmagazine.com/material-handling-dry-wet/conveyors/article/55140313/bucket-elevator-basics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Processing</a>). Buckets are spaced apart on the belt, with gaps between buckets. As each bucket passes over the head pulley, centrifugal force throws the material out of the bucket and into the discharge chute. The material trajectory follows a parabolic path from the bucket lip to the discharge opening.</p>
<h3>Operating Principle</h3>
<p>At the boot (bottom), buckets scoop material from the boot pit or receive material from an infeed chute. The loaded buckets travel up the vertical leg. At the head (top), the high belt speed generates enough centrifugal force to overcome gravity as the bucket inverts around the head pulley. The material releases from the bucket and flies into the discharge throat. Proper discharge depends on the relationship between belt speed, head pulley diameter, and material characteristics.</p>
<h3>Best Applications</h3>
<p>Centrifugal discharge suits free-flowing, non-fragile materials: grain, dry sand, cement, sugar, salt, and plastic pellets. The high speed and centrifugal discharge generate some material impact and degradation, making this type less suitable for fragile products. Centrifugal elevators deliver the highest capacity per belt width because the buckets move faster. The same Kansas State article cites capacities up to about 150 tons per hour for centrifugal units, and larger high-capacity legs exist. Actual capacity depends on bucket size, spacing, belt speed, and bulk density. Centrifugal elevators dominate grain elevator applications. KWS notes that centrifugal units dig material from the boot, so some designs add reinforced digger buckets at intervals (<a href="https://www.kwsmfg.com/resources/ask-the-experts/centrifugal-vs-continuous/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KWS Manufacturing</a>).</p>
<h3>Belt vs Chain</h3>
<p>Centrifugal discharge elevators use either rubber/PVC belting or roller chain to carry the buckets. Belt elevators are quieter, lighter, and usually cost less. Chain elevators handle higher temperatures, heavier materials, and more abrasive conditions. Belt elevators dominate grain and food applications. Chain elevators serve cement, mining, and heavy industrial applications.</p>
<h2 id="continuous-heading">Continuous Discharge Elevators</h2>
<p>Continuous discharge bucket elevators run at lower speed with buckets mounted close together. KWS Manufacturing gives typical speeds of 100 to 150 fpm for continuous elevators, about half the speed of centrifugal units. Material leaves each bucket by gravity and slides down the back of the preceding bucket, whose extended sides act as a chute, creating a continuous flow of material from the buckets into the discharge chute.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/bucket-kws-head-cutaway.webp" width="600" height="400" alt="Cutaway rendering of the top of a bucket elevator showing closely spaced cream-colored buckets passing over the head shaft inside a gray casing, with a blue motor and yellow drive guard on the side." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Cutaway render of the head section of a KWS continuous bucket elevator. The buckets are mounted close together, and the drive sits on a platform beside the head shaft. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.kwsmfg.com/resources/problem-solvers/bucket-elevator-for-catalyst-processing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KWS Manufacturing</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Gentle Handling</h3>
<p>The lower speed and gravity discharge minimize material impact and degradation. Continuous discharge elevators handle fragile materials: breakfast cereals, snack foods, pet food kibble, pharmaceutical tablets, and decorative coatings. Breakage is lower than in centrifugal elevators because material is poured rather than thrown. Continuous discharge elevators are usually gentler than <a href="/conveyor-types/screw-conveyors/">vertical screw conveyors</a> and dilute-phase pneumatic systems for friable products.</p>
<h3>Sluggish Materials</h3>
<p>Continuous discharge also handles materials that do not release cleanly from buckets under centrifugal force: damp powders, sticky materials, and materials with high angle of repose. The slow inversion and gravity pour provide more time for complete bucket emptying. Bucket design variations (round-bottom, V-shaped, flared) optimize discharge for specific material characteristics.</p>
<h3>Capacity</h3>
<p>Continuous discharge elevators handle lower capacity per belt width than centrifugal types because the lower belt speed means fewer bucket loads per minute. Compensate by using wider belts, deeper buckets, or running two elevators in parallel. For applications where material quality matters more than throughput rate, continuous discharge is the standard choice.</p>
<h2 id="internal-heading">Positive and Internal Discharge Elevators</h2>
<p>Positive discharge elevators mount spaced buckets between two strands of chain. Below the head sprockets, the chain passes over a snub sprocket that bends the strand back and turns each bucket fully over, emptying it. Spirotech describes this as a variation on gravity discharge using twin-strand chain with large buckets, at typical chain speeds of 0.5 to 0.7 m/s, roughly 100 to 140 fpm (<a href="https://www.spirotechgroup.co.uk/products/bulk-materials-handling/bucket-elevators/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spirotech</a>). The design suits light, lumpy, sticky, or fragile products.</p>
<p>Other discharge names, such as internal discharge, are not used consistently between manufacturers, so confirm the actual discharge mechanism on the drawing rather than relying on the name.</p>
<h3>Complete Discharge</h3>
<p>Full inversion empties the bucket more completely than centrifugal or gravity discharge, leaving little residual material. This matters for high-value materials, materials that build up on bucket walls, and operations requiring minimal cross-contamination between batches. Pharmaceutical, chemical, and specialty food operations use internal discharge elevators.</p>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<p>Positive discharge elevators use twin-strand chain and a snub sprocket, which adds cost and parts compared with single-belt or single-chain designs. Capacity is moderate, similar to continuous discharge types. They are specified when discharge completeness justifies the added cost, typically in batch operations where residual material between batches is unacceptable.</p>
