<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a bucket elevator?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Buckets on a belt or chain loop inside an enclosed casing, lifting bulk material vertically. Discharge types: centrifugal (high speed, free-flowing), continuous (gentle, fragile), positive (twin chain, buckets fully inverted). See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types guide</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Centrifugal vs continuous discharge, which is better?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Centrifugal for free-flowing, non-fragile materials at high capacity. Continuous for fragile, sluggish, or damp materials needing gentle handling. Centrifugal costs less and delivers higher TPH. Continuous preserves material quality. Match to your material requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How high can a bucket elevator lift?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on design and manufacturer. Grain terminals, cement plants, and mines use the tallest elevators. Chain elevators handle heavier, hotter, and more abrasive material than belt elevators. Lift height drives belt tension, casing height, structural support, and drive horsepower, so confirm maximum centers for a given model with the supplier.</p>

</div>
</div>
